BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Shabaqo Stone
DTSTART:20260923T000000
DTEND:20260923T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:ca300978f53502595806b95141c43f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The Shabaqo Stone is a monument that preserves some of the mos
 t important religious writings to survive from ancient Egypt. It was carve
 d for Shabaqo\, a Kushite king who ruled Egypt during the Twenty-Fifth Dyn
 asty (ca. 722–655 BCE). One of its central texts is the so‑called “M
 emphite Theology\,” which portrays the god Ptah as a universal creator w
 ho brings the world into being through the powers of thought and speech. I
 n this talk\, Joshua Roberson will introduce the Shabaqo Stone’s histori
 cal and religious significance\, and reveal a behind-the-scenes look at th
 e first-ever digital documentation of this remarkable monument\, carried o
 ut at the British Museum in 2019. Explore new insights\, recent discoverie
 s\, and the cutting‑edge technologies that are transforming our understa
 nding of one of Egypt’s most fascinating theological texts. LEARN MORE /
  GET TICKETS\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.c
 om/event/the-shabaqo-stone/92866/
LOCATION:Geological Lecture Hall\, Geological Lecture Hall\, Harvard Museu
 m of the Ancient Near East 24 Oxford Street\, Cambridge\, MA\, 02138
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-shabaqo-stone/92866/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Way We Were: Roxbury Black Wall Street\, 1900-1965
DTSTART:20260923T000000
DTEND:20260924T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:523364fedb1dce4af37281b44977a3@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The Way We Were returns Sept. 23 & 24th! Free admission. RSVP 
 today to reserve your seat and explore Roxbury Black Wall Street The conve
 rsation doesn't end here...it continues. After an incredible gathering at 
 Billie Holiday on the Vineyard\, we invite you to join us for The Way We W
 ere: Roxbury Black Wall Street 1900–1965 Conference II. For two days\, h
 istorians\, researchers\, community leaders\, archivists\, and descendants
  will come together to explore one of the most significant yet overlooked 
 stories in American history. Through original research\, oral histories\, 
 personal narratives\, and thought-provoking discussions\, we'll examine ho
 w Roxbury\, Tulsa\, and Oak Bluffs became interconnected centers of Black 
 excellence\, entrepreneurship\, culture\, and economic power. This isn't s
 imply about remembering the past. It's about reclaiming a history that hel
 ps shape our future. 📍 Shaw Nubian Library Nubian Square\, Boston\, MA 
 📅 September 23–24\, 2026 🎟️ Admission is FREE\, but seating is l
 imited. We encourage everyone to RSVP early to reserve your seat and be pa
 rt of this important conversation. Come learn. Come question. Come connect
 . Most importantly\, come help preserve the stories that deserve to be tol
 d.\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/t
 he-way-we-were%3a-roxbury-black-wall-street-1900-1965/92597/
LOCATION:Various Locations\, Boston\, Boston\, MA
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-way-we-were%3a-roxbury-black-wall
 -street-1900-1965/92597/
END:VEVENT
