BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Larger Landscape Conversation: Living Collections
DTSTART:20260924T000000
DTEND:20260924T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:8efcb871f0e10cc4ec3f2ca5330d69@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:When you think of an art museum\, you probably think of painti
 ngs and sculptures. But what about plants? Just as the Courtyard is at the
  heart of the Gardner\, other museums also curate flora and fauna—or “
 living collections”—just as much as they do artworks. But how do museu
 ms balance the needs of a 16th-century oil painting alongside a humidity-l
 oving orchid? Is a museum obligated to give historical plant installations
  such as the Gardner’s Hanging Nasturtiums the same amount of care as a 
 Van Gogh or a Botticelli? How can plants be cultivated alongside conserved
  works of art? Join us for an exploration of these questions and more with
  three experts in the care of plants alongside artwork: Erika Rumbley\, th
 e Gardner’s Stanley P. Kozak Director of Horticulture\; Christina Nielse
 n of The Huntington\; and Maeve Turner of The Frick Collection. The Larger
  Landscape Conversation is hosted by Charles Waldheim\, Harvard Graduate S
 chool of Design Professor of Landscape Architecture and the Gardner Museum
 ’s Ruettgers Curator of Landscape. LEARN MORE / GET TICKETS\n\nSources: 
 Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-larger-lands
 cape-conversation%3a-living-collections/93004/
LOCATION:Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum\, Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum
  25 Evans Way\, Boston\, MA\, 02215
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-larger-landscape-conversation%3a-
 living-collections/93004/
END:VEVENT
