BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Provincetown Book Festival
DTSTART:20260925T000000
DTEND:20260927T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:caa27646a7d7136015500fd554506e@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The 2026 Provincetown Book Festival\, scheduled for September 
 25 – 27\, features prestigious authors Ibram X. Kendi and Hannah Lillith
  Assadi. Kendi\, author of the bestselling How to Be an Antiracist and mos
 t recently \, Chain of Ideas \, will be interviewed by Dorothy E. Roberts
 \, Professor of Civil Rights at the University of Pennsylvania\, at 6:30 p
 .m. on Saturday\, September 26. Hannah Lillith Assadi is the author of Par
 adise 17 \, a novel inspired by her Palestinian father’s search for home
 . She will appear at 9:00 a.m. Saturday\, September 26\, along with Nation
 al Book Award-winning novelist Julia Glass\, who will interview Assadi. Th
 e Provincetown Book Festival opens on Friday night\, September 19\, at 6:0
 0 p.m.\, with the Rose Dorothea Award Reading and Reception at the Provinc
 etown Library. The Rose Dorothea Award will be presented to David Dunlap\,
  author of the book Building Provincetown\, which explores Provincetown hi
 story through its architecture. The Rose Dorothea Award is given annually 
 by the Board of Library Trustees to someone with a strong connection to th
 e Outer Cape who has made a significant contribution through the written w
 ord. Sixteen other prominent authors will appear as guests at the Festival
 \, including local favorite Russ Lopez\, author of Provincetown Stories\, 
 who will be in conversation with Michael Lowenthal\, author of a book of e
 ssays called Place Envy \; and Christopher Castelanni\, author of the true
  crime-inspired novel Last Seen \, along with American Book Award winner D
 e’Shawn Charles Winslow\, author of the novel The Fervent Whites. For mo
 re information about all of these programs\, including appearances by LGBT
 Q+ pioneering poets Robin Becker and Acie Clark\; and many wonderful novel
 ists like Pema Aguda and Naima Coster\, go to provincetownbookfestival.org
 . All programs are free and open to the public\, and reservations are avai
 lable online. All Festival events take place at the Provincetown Library\,
  356 Commercial St. ASL interpretation for the deaf and hard of hearing is
  available by request. The deadline for requests is September 9. The Provi
 ncetown Book Festival is supported in part by the Friends of the Provincet
 own Library\, the Massachusetts Center for the Book\, the Massachusetts Cu
 ltural Council and Provincetown Cultural Council\, and the Provincetown To
 urism Fund.\n\nSources: Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodc
 hamber.org/event/provincetown-book-festival/52158/
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/provincetown-book-festival/52158/
END:VEVENT
