BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Children's Tumor Foundation
DTSTART:20260926T000000
DTEND:20260926T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:dd3ced4d69d9597c5815b7262a43ed@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:NF Walk (formerly Shine a Light NF Walk) is the signature fund
 raising event of the Children’s Tumor Foundation. Our goal is to raise a
 wareness of neurofibromatosis and schwannomatosis\, genetic conditions tha
 t collectively affect more than 4 million people worldwide and raise criti
 cal funds to advance research and treatments. Held in communities across t
 he United States\, NF Walk events are family-friendly gatherings where peo
 ple living with NF\, their families\, and supporters can come together\, c
 onnect\, and help drive progress toward better treatments and a cure. The 
 NF Walk is how we go faster. Faster research. Faster treatments. Faster to
  the finish line for the millions living with NF. Walk with us so that tre
 atments exist when patients need them. NF Walk brings the community togeth
 er to raise awareness\, support families\, and move us closer to ending NF
 . LEARN MORE\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.c
 om/event/childrens-tumor-foundation/92452/
LOCATION:Next to the Robert Greene Memorial at Castle Island\, 2010 Willia
 m J Day Blvd\, Boston\, MA\, 02127
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/childrens-tumor-foundation/92452/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Choate Island Day
DTSTART:20260926T100000
DTEND:20260926T150000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:e4972c3d66049420a27551c849b8d6@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Spend a day with us in celebration of Choate Island – the cr
 own jewel of the Crane Wildlife Refuge. Here\, deer and foxes roam through
  fields that once housed herds of sheep\, and osprey fly over breathtaking
  views of Essex Bay. The island was first home to indigenous farmers and f
 ishers\, then to generations of the Choate family\, and was later a retrea
 t for Cornelius and Miné Crane\, who loved the place so much they decided
  to be buried at the summit. This year\, we are again offering both a Self
 -Guided and Guided experience. Both will allow visitors to explore the ext
 ent of the island. The Guided experience will be led by a trained guide fo
 r the first 90 minutes of the program on a more thoroughly interpreted wal
 k across the island\, after which visitors will be free to take their time
  getting back to the boat dock. The Guided experience starts at the island
 -side boat dock and ends at the Choate Island summit. However\, we will ha
 ve staff and volunteers scattered across the island to provide interpretat
 ion for self-guided visitors as well. See ticketing options below for Guid
 ed and Self-Guided tours. Park at Crane Beach\, which is at the end of Arg
 illa Road. Parking will be free for this event. Board our shuttle bus to t
 ake you straight down to the boat dock (less than 5-minute drive). There y
 ou can check in (if you purchased tickets online) or buy tickets “at the
  door” (if available) before boarding the ferry to the island\, which is
  less than a 10-minute boat trip one-way. The shuttles will run continuous
 ly between 10AM and 3PM. The last boat returns from the island at 3PM. Bel
 ow is a sampling of events for the day (subject to change): Talks on Choat
 e Island life\, human\, and natural history Proctor Barn open house Choate
  House self-guided tours Hikes to the Crane Burial Site Self-guided walkin
 g tour Kids scavenger hunt Shoreline walk and beachcombing Light refreshme
 nts will be served. Feel free to bring a picnic lunch but remember to carr
 y out all that you carry in\, and please\, no alcohol. Ticket Options: Cho
 ate Island Day – Guided Experience (Morning) 10AM-12:30PM Nonmember Adul
 t $50/Member Adult $40\, Nonmember Child $35/Member Child $28 Choate Islan
 d Day – Guided Experience (afternoon) 12:30PM-3PM Nonmember Adult $50/Me
 mber Adult $40\, Nonmember Child $35/Member Child $28 Choate Island Day- S
 elf-Guided Tour Nonmember Adult $40/Member Adult $32\, Nonmember Child $30
 /Member Child $24 (Photo: Stoney Stone/Trustees)\n\nSources: North of Bost
 on\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/choate-island-day-2/
LOCATION:The Crane Estate\, 310 Argilla Road\, Ipswich\, MA\, 01938
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/choate-island-day-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Chromatic Wood Type — Old and New Interactive Discussion at the 
 Museum of Printing
DTSTART:20260926T130000
DTEND:20260926T160000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:a9cc570fec3c649573d9b87b1fd78a@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Geri McCormick and James Grieshaber will give an explanation o
 f chromatic wood type and how they have recreated the making process to re
 vive historic chromatic fonts at Virgin Wood Type in Rochester\, NY. Their
  rediscovery of the process has led to an all new design which debuted ear
 lier this year called Marquee. There will be an informal activity followin
 g the talk where attendees will be able to pull a proof of a historic Will
 iam H. Page chromatic wood type ca. 1849 and the new Marquee font from Vir
 gin Wood Type made just this year. About the Museum of Printing The Museum
  of Printing is a non-profit dedicated to preserving the rich history of t
 he graphic arts\, printing and typesetting technology\, and printing craft
 smanship. In addition to many special collections and small exhibits\, the
  Museum contains hundreds of antique printing\, typesetting\, and bindery 
 machines\, as well as a library of books and printing-related documents an
 d a recently added bible room. The Museum is located at 15 Thornton Avenue
  in Haverhill\, Massachusetts. You can find us on the web at museumofprint
 ing.org and follow our social media activity on Facebook ( TheMuseumofPrin
 ting )\, Instagram ( museumofprinting )\, Twitter ( @MOPrinting )\, Substa
 ck\, Bluesky ( @museumofprinting.bsky.social) \, and YouTube ( @museumofpr
 inting ).\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.o
 rg/event/chromatic-wood-type-old-and-new-interactive-discussion-at-the-mus
 eum-of-printing/
LOCATION:Museum of Printing\, 15 Thornton Ave\, Haverhill\, MA\, 01832
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/chromatic-wood-type-old-and-new-intera
 ctive-discussion-at-the-museum-of-printing/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Chuseok Festival
DTSTART:20260926T000000
DTEND:20260926T000000
DTSTAMP:20260923T155014Z
UID:1245ab52d44111140c373556074f11@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The Chuseok Festival celebrates one of Korea’s most importan
 t cultural holidays\, centered around the harvest\, gratitude and family r
 eunions. Explore traditional and contemporary expressions of Korean cultur
 e through storytelling\, art making and a curator-led tour! This multigene
 rational celebration also welcomes first-time and returning visitors to PE
 M’s Korean Art gallery. PEM Pals: Chuseok Stories with Cori Ahn 10–11 
 am | Main Atrium\, Ground Level A family-focused storytelling and art-maki
 ng program introduces younger audiences to Chuseok traditions\, with their
  themes of harvest and family connection. Storyteller Cori Ahn will also s
 how audiences a display of hanbok\, Korean traditional clothing. Large-Sca
 le Hangeul Calligraphy Demonstration with Youngsun Jang and Hannah Jang 1
 –2 pm | Main Atrium\, Ground Level Experience a live Hangeul calligraphy
  demonstration that reveals the beauty\, structure and expressive qualitie
 s of the Korean writing system through large-scale brushwork. The featured
  phrase\, 늘 한가위만 같아라 ("May every day be like Hangawi")\, e
 xpresses a traditional wish for a life filled with abundance\, gratitude a
 nd the joy of sharing with others. Inspired by the spirit of Hangawi\, the
  Korean harvest festival\, the demonstration highlights the enduring cultu
 ral values of generosity\, community and ensuring that everyone has enough
 . Visitors will also have the opportunity to write their own Chuseok messa
 ges on small scrolls to take home. Drop-In Art Making | Korean Culture thr
 ough Contemporary Fashion and Design with Cori Ahn 11 am–3 pm | Create S
 pace Studios\, Ground Level Teens and adults are invited to explore Korean
  culture through fashion and visual expression. Storyteller Cori Ahn will 
 also show audiences a display of hanbok\, Korean traditional clothing. Cur
 ator-Led Tour: Yu Kil-Chun Gallery of Korean Art and Culture 2–2:45 pm |
  Korean Art gallery\, Level 2 Visit the Korean Art gallery with Curator Ji
 yeon Kim to see how PEM’s historical Korean works from the 1890s and bey
 ond connect to contemporary Korean popular culture. This tour draws interp
 retive links between traditional Korean art and modern cultural references
 . Chuseok Selfie Station 10 am–4 pm Snap a selfie to celebrate your day 
 at PEM! Chuseok Menu in the Atrium Café 10 am–5 pm Enjoy a seasonal men
 u of Chuseok-inspired food and drinks highlighting cultural references and
  Korean harvest traditions. The Chuseok Festival is supported by the Consu
 late General of the Republic of Korea in Boston.\n\nSources: Meet Boston\n
 Event page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/chuseok-festival/92617/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, 161 Essex Street\, Salem\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/chuseok-festival/92617/
END:VEVENT
